\paragraph{Limits.}
The run sets are not random: a model's observed cells are the tasks its authors chose to run, which may differ from the tasks a new model's evaluator can run, and the target scores are single measurements. The temporal and strength splits rest on 13 and 22 models from 4 and 11 organisations, so what they say about extrapolation is indicative. The factor model is linear in the latent factors; the real matrix is outside every single misspecification we tried, and the cause of the factor $2.5$ between the measured and predicted floor is not identified, so the paper does not claim that a better inference rule, rather than a better run set, would lower the error. The greedy rule is not guaranteed to find the best run set, and the one interview-style baseline we ran (S7) is not an adaptive version of it. The results are for embedding models on MTEB and make no statement about other kinds of model. Shorter run sets save evaluation compute; the risk is that a score inferred from a short run set is read as a measurement, which the floor of about five points does not support.

We use the public MTEB results~\citep{muennighoff2023mteb} at one pinned commit of the results repository (Section~\ref{sec:release}). A benchmark is one text task; a cell is a model's main score on it, in points on a 0--100 scale. We keep one revision per model and the modal dataset revision and language set per task, drop configuration variants and duplicate model names, and keep tasks with at least 50 models and models with at least 30 tasks, iterated to a fixed point. The result is a matrix of $M=238$ models and $N=328$ tasks with 45{,}775 observed cells (41\% missing) and 83 organisations (Appendix~\ref{app:data}, Table~\ref{tab:coverage}). Of the tasks, 117 are retrieval and 73 classification: MTEB tasks are fine-grained, so $N$ is large relative to the number of task types. The coverage thresholds were chosen after viewing counts of models and tasks, not scores.

\paragraph{Held-out units and splits.}
The unit of hold-out is the organisation: models from one organisation are fitted together and held out together, since a lab's models share training data and recipes and a split by model would let a sibling stand in for the new model. The 83 organisations are assigned to 10 folds, and every model is held out once, with standardisation, factor model, rank, and margins fitted on the other folds only (the group split, G). Two further splits stress the setting: a temporal split trains on the 70\% earliest-released dated models and tests on the later ones (T; 68 test models from 37 organisations), and a strength split trains on the weakest 75\% by an additive model offset and tests on the strongest quarter (S; 60 test models from 32 organisations). Organisations overlap between train and test in T.

