\paragraph{Shape of the curve.}
Let $\tilde V_S$ be the rows $v_j/\sqrt{\psi_j}$, $j\in S$. Since $\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}(S)=(I+\tilde V_S^{\top}\tilde V_S)^{-1}\preceq\bigl(1+\lambda_{\min}(\tilde V_S^{\top}\tilde V_S)\bigr)^{-1}I$,
\begin{equation}
D_T(S)\le\frac{\overline{\|v\|^2}_T}{1+\lambda_{\min}(\tilde V_S^{\top}\tilde V_S)} .
\end{equation}
The bound separates two regimes. If $S$ cannot span the $r$ factors, $\lambda_{\min}=0$ and $D_T$ stays near $\overline{\|v\|^2}_T$ (it falls only along the directions the run set covers, so a few well-chosen benchmarks help most). Once $S$ spans them, the bound falls as $1/(1+ck)$ whenever $\lambda_{\min}$ grows at least as fast as $ck$, which holds for a random run set when the loadings are spread out rather than concentrated on a few benchmarks. With separated factors (eigenvalues well above $\psi$) the transition is a knee near the number of factors; with a spectrum that decays slowly it is a gradual closure, and the constant depends on how spread out the loadings are. Both regimes are tested on synthetic data in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}.

The needed run-set size follows from the excess over the floor in RMSE, which is the quantity of Section~\ref{sec:protocol}. With $F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(S)$ as above, the model predicts
\begin{equation}
\text{excess}(k)=\sqrt{F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(S_k)}-\sqrt{F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(C)},
\end{equation}
and $k^*_{\mathrm{pred}}(\epsilon)$ is the smallest $k$ at which this is at most $\epsilon$. It is a prediction from the training spectrum and noise alone, with no use of the measured floor, and we test it against the measured $k^*(\epsilon)$ rather than assume it.

\begin{proposition}[Adaptive and batch selection coincide]\label{prop:t3}
For known $V,\psi$ and Gaussian noise, with squared loss and a fixed number of benchmarks, the posterior covariance $\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}(S)$ does not depend on the revealed scores. An adaptive policy, which chooses the next benchmark from the scores revealed so far, therefore reaches the same expected posterior variance as the best fixed run set of the same size.
\end{proposition}
\noindent\emph{Proof.} $\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}(S)$ is a function of $S$ alone. The value of any adaptive policy is an average over score histories of $F_T(S)$ for the set $S$ it ends with, so no policy beats $\min_{|S|=k}F_T(S)$, and the batch minimiser attains it. \qed

\noindent The proposition does not say adaptive selection is useless when the model is wrong; whether it helps when the model is wrong is not tested here.
